  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SAVING THE GOLDEN-CHEEKED WARBLER WINTER</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Alliance for the Conservation of Mesoamerican Pine-Oak Forests will follow up on the implementation of the &apos;Conservation Plan for the Central American Pine-Oak Forest Ecoregion and the Golden-cheeked Warbler (GCWA)&apos;, specifically focusing on two strategies to be implemented in the Highlands of Chiapas in Mexico, Sierra de las Minas Biosphere Reserve in Guatemala, and Misoco and Opalaca Biological Reserves in Honduras: Recovery of the winter habitat of the GCWA in degraded areas and Integrated Fire Management (IFM). The restoration of the winter habitat of the GCWA on a landscape scale continues to be a need at the regional level, for this reason partners will establish 13 nurseries to produce 1,250,000 native plants, which will be used to restore 1,145 hectares. In addition, each country will select one site to collect information on the avifauna that inhabit or make use of these areas. The management and protection of pine-oak forests to reduce the threat of forest fires is also a priority, for this reason capacity building will be strengthened at the local level to learn more about the IFM approach for the development of 6 IFM plans, the formation of 6 forestry brigades (2 in each country), the purchase of basic equipment for the future development of field activities that will help protect 600 hectares of forest. Finally, it is important to continue the strengthening of the Alliance and monitoring the implementation of the Conservation Plan so partners will create a tool for monitoring and evaluating the progress and achievements of the Conservation Plan, which can be adopted by the partners to report on the established goals.</narrative>
  </description>
